{"step_index":594,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-08T22:38:36Z","input_tokens":3378,"cache_read_tokens":125479,"output_tokens":974,"thinking":"The instruction to provide the downloaded file or result code to the instructor is found exclusively within the HTML file associated with the specific quiz (QUIZ06). This suggests the expected user action involves downloading a result file and then providing it to the instructor, or providing a result code. A download button is present in the main index file, as well.\n\n\nThe initial focus was on modifying the student-facing quiz interface based on auto-submission settings. The intent shifted to implementing auto-recording functionality in the teacher interface (`teacher.html`). This involves updating the settings state, connecting a button to the auto-record setting, and ensuring the setting is saved to persistent storage.\n\n\nIf auto-recording is enabled, the result screen instructions must be updated to reflect automatic submission. Conversely, if auto-recording is disabled, the instructions should remain unchanged, referencing the download file or result code requirement. Confirmed that the start screen instructions do not require modification.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"search_web","args":{"query":"\"student quiz submission confirmation ux copy feedback text web\""}}]}
{"step_index":595,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-08T22:38:45Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-09T06:38:45+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-09T06:38:49+08:00\nThe search for \"student quiz submission confirmation ux copy feedback text web\" returned the following summary:\nDesigning the UX copy and feedback text for a **student quiz submission confirmation** requires a delicate balance. Students are often anxious about whether their work went through, if their answers saved correctly, and when they will see a grade. \n\nDepending on your product’s architecture and grading policy, here is a breakdown of UX copy best practices, structure patterns, and ready-to-use examples for web applications.\n\n---\n\n### Key Elements of a Great Quiz Submission State\n1. **Immediate Reassurance:** Address the primary user anxiety right away (*\"Did it actually submit?\"*). Use a clear visual indicator like a green checkmark alongside the text[1].\n2. **Set Clear Expectations:** Tell the student what happens next (e.g., *Are scores visible immediately? Will the instructor review it manually?*)[2][1].\n3. **Provide a Safe Exit:** Give a clear path forward, such as returning to the course dashboard or viewing results (if permitted)[3].\n\n---\n\n### UX Copy Options by Scenario\n\n#### Scenario A: Automated Grading (Instant Score Available)\n*Best for multiple-choice or auto-graded quizzes where students can see results right away.*\n\n* **Header/Title:** Quiz submitted\n* **Body Text:** You answered 8 out of 10 questions correctly. Your score has been saved to your gradebook.\n* **Primary CTA:** [View detailed results]\n* **Secondary CTA:** [Return to course]\n\n#### Scenario B: Manual Instructor Grading (No Instant Score)\n*Best for essay-heavy quizzes, open-ended questions, or instances where scores are hidden until a deadline passes[2][4].*\n\n* **Header/Title:** Quiz submitted successfully\n* **Body Text:** Your professor will review your answers[2]. You’ll receive a notification when your grade is posted.\n* **Primary CTA:** [Return to dashboard]\n\n#### Scenario C: Timed/High-Stakes Exams (Strict Closure)\n*Best for midterms or finals w\n<truncated 1073 bytes>\nCelebration:** Unless it’s a gamified learning module, avoid words like *\"Amazing!\"* or *\"Woohoo!\"* for routine academic quiz submissions[3][1]. Keep the tone encouraging, calm, and professional.\n* **Avoid \"Success!\" as a standalone title:** Instead, name the exact action that succeeded (e.g., *\"Quiz submitted\"* or *\"Assignment received\"*)[3][1]. \n* **Timestamp the submission:** If technical glitches are a common concern on your platform, displaying a precise timestamp (*\"Submitted today at 3:14 PM\"*) drastically reduces student support tickets.\n\nSources:\n[1] [mintlify.app](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQH4VzJpSzsX5Gb_JdJ3apLRhRh6lx2ULYo8k_HvlvSTUhfNhcsz0SF_qIL9DeGBFJhIqiPlyKXR0iJPX-cnaBrUaXf3Y92RYKM6bYZN1vJOAlVZiRu6AXwyiUS8AT9XSIIxAlPhSq9r8zm1UwOhkPuAzgkF9qZUvCCH2JTFlQKvZ26ktfKF3n8=)\n[2] [instructure.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQE8C4l2Ql5U-JzTDeGNQwND1QNsW8hC_D5ncVVIQEOC1Ytgr6BJPWXfPWdhyJZuTuY0wmfizZV_V9rPiCzMgHWpHYPNpWivGJlQDBqfOZxJ6lQNtvHoSDAEu2jNhM29T0m1K_mtneWCA6sJmexDPQptavOL0UmoPpa43HNnbo60hFAdXW8ka2AhH7D7xNrddJntFrde_HMW7QlPts3_vv5y_o82To7O-I6-Ux7LNJDbI2MUTe7n)\n[3] [atlassian.design](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGlYP0u3vvg6coaCZHNcJ0DLZEdDcU4lz3FUQOaAx_Vdp-sYPBYUYT4_ubh9DWQ_OVCNEF3xkhdQk_HdXoKDtX-dBbT2TfkscAVzWxAGBx1PphJEyhr8hGjtSmAodG9xQxxdGmhITDl0aCdhJpaD9ppaAVsUXTSK6fNseQAIC2kxC-Oy_YE-Q==)\n[4] [edublogs.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHRv78wyofxqZmRvFfvTqz4WVKsfz-0Mvmcbb4_POzq7JZTyUpj4cdrUIQ8f7U0xjrmL1GvPmwjDqedFvkkC8oIYKC8J6cOF3GXtdpLs-fD9nwlBwhyknI7StaaVTQP4D19S2OSEsxkRrGXRIgYq56aWQnFDSoruyHtPLUQsFYNhFX10xxdrARF2g==)\n[5] [pencilandpaper.io](https://ver